//
// A view of a struct-ENUM's payload (`x = sh.inner`) whose SUBJECT is reassigned while the
// view is still live is copied at the bind and the author is told — the same answer the
// plain-struct twin has always given.
//
// `(B-Disturb)` in formal/binding.md names reassigning the container as one of the three
// events that END the place a view names, and `(B-View)` says the binding MATERIALISES there:
// it is given its own copy, taken at the bind, and the author is told.  A payload view got
// neither.  It answered the REASSIGNED subject's bytes at the payload's offset on the
// interpreter — `Empty { z: 0 }` read as the old `S`, so `x.a` was `0` — and the old payload
// on `--native`, which is the right value reached by a different route; nothing was said on
// either backend, and no copy was made.
//
// The cause is one derivation short of one shape.  A payload projection does not name its
// subject directly: `sh.inner` lowers to `OpGetField(if <tag == Holder> { sh } else
// { OpNullRefSentinel() }, …)`, so the accessor's first argument is a variant-check `if`
// rather than a `Var`, and the chain that peels a projection to its container variable
// bottomed out at `None`.  That chain had TWO byte-identical copies, each documented as
// "mirroring" the other — `scopes::base_container_var`, which the walk that NAMES a view to
// materialise reads, and `generation::container_element_base`, which the emitters that COPY
// it read — so the peel had to be written twice to work once.  Both now call
// `use_analysis::projection_container_var`, beside the `is_projection_op` list whose own doc
// already names them as readers of one shape.
//
// ⚠ The advice is half the fix and this file cannot see it (a `.loft` test reads values, not
// diagnostics).  `tests/docs` and the copy-manifest gates carry that half; what is pinned
// here is that both backends now answer the OLD payload, which is what the copy means.
//
// @falsified-at: 5f4ac074 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 2 assertion failures -> 0; native
//   INERT, which is expected of a backend-divergence guard: only one side can move.  Native
//   answered the old payload already, so the VALUE channel moves on the interpreter alone and
//   what the fix buys is that the two AGREE.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.

struct Pay { a: integer, b: text }

enum Subj { Holder { inner: Pay }, Empty { z: integer } }

struct Twin { inner: Pay, t: text }

fn mk_pay(n: integer) -> Pay { Pay{a: n, b: "n{n}"} }

// THE DEFECT — the subject is reassigned while the payload view is live.
fn test_a_payload_view_survives_its_subjects_reassignment() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk_pay(1)};
  x = sh.inner;
  sh = Empty{z: 0};
  assert(x.a == 1, "the view keeps the payload it was taken from: got {x.a}, want 1");
}

// The same shape one variant over: reassigned to the SAME variant with a different payload.
fn test_a_payload_view_survives_a_same_variant_reassignment() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk_pay(1)};
  x = sh.inner;
  sh = Holder{inner: mk_pay(7)};
  assert(x.a == 1, "still the payload it was taken from: got {x.a}, want 1");
}

// The plain-struct twin, which has materialised and said so since @PLN130 F8 — the control
// that says this fix made the enum agree with the struct rather than inventing a rule.
fn test_the_plain_struct_twin_is_unchanged() {
  tw = Twin{inner: mk_pay(1), t: "a"};
  x = tw.inner;
  tw = Twin{inner: mk_pay(5), t: "b"};
  assert(x.a == 1, "struct twin: got {x.a}, want 1");
}

// CONTROL — an UNDISTURBED payload view still ALIASES, which is what `(B-View)` is for.  A
// fix that copied every payload view would pass every cell above and fail this one.
//
// It is also loft#1395's shape: the `lost-write` WARNING claimed this write was lost, because
// the oracle could not name the payload projection's base and read the view as an OWNER.  The
// binding is read back here as well as written, which keeps the cell honest about the alias
// without depending on that warning's absence.
fn test_an_undisturbed_payload_view_still_aliases() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk_pay(1)};
  x = sh.inner;
  x.a = 9;
  assert(x.a == 9, "the view reads its own write: got {x.a}, want 9");
  assert(sh.inner.a == 9, "a live view writes through: got {sh.inner.a}, want 9");
}

// CONTROL — a payload view whose last use PRECEDES the reassignment is not at risk and must
// not be copied; it reads the value it always did.
fn test_a_view_used_before_the_reassignment_is_untouched() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk_pay(3)};
  x = sh.inner;
  got = x.a;
  sh = Empty{z: 0};
  assert(got == 3, "read before the reassignment: got {got}, want 3");
}

// CONTROL — a MUTATION of the subject's payload is not a disturbance (`(B-Disturb)`: writing
// INTO a place a view names is not ending it), so the view sees the write.
fn test_a_write_into_the_payload_is_not_a_disturbance() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk_pay(1)};
  x = sh.inner;
  sh.inner.a = 4;
  assert(x.a == 4, "a write into the place reaches the view: got {x.a}, want 4");
}
